Subject: [GTK][WPE] Read back only the changed area of a shared memory render target

Draft against the webkitgtk-2.52.6 release tarball. Apply with -p1 from the
tarball root, AFTER 0002 (the context of one hunk comes from it). Independent
of 0001 and 0004. Compiled and measured (dev/bench/RESULTS.md).

What it does
  RenderTargetSHMImage::didRenderFrame() called glReadPixels() on the whole
  surface for every frame. The damage record of a render target already says
  what changed since that target was last presented, and the shared bitmap of
  the target still holds the pixels of that presentation, so only the bounds
  of the record have to be read back.

  - The record is now maintained on every frame that has frame damage, whether
    or not UseDamagingInformationForCompositing is on (before, targets only
    accumulated damage through renderTargetDamage(), which only the
    compositing flag calls).
  - AcceleratedSurface::didRenderFrame() converts the bounds to buffer
    coordinates (Y flipped when shouldPaintMirrored(), which is the GTK 3
    case) and hands them to the target before resetting the record.
  - The sub-rectangle is read in place with GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH. On a context
    older than OpenGL (ES) 3.0 it falls back to full-width rows, which need no
    row length.
  - No frame damage for the frame (damage propagation off) or an unknown
    record (new target, resize) still reads the whole surface.

What it does not handle
  - It trusts the frame damage to be exact. Anything drawn outside the
    reported damage (antialiased layer edges, a debug overlay) would stay
    stale in the shared bitmap. WEBKIT_SHOW_DAMAGE reports full damage, so it
    is safe.
  - The bounds are one rectangle: scattered damage reads their bounding box.
  - It assumes 4 bytes per pixel, as the previous call did.
  - The fence wait before the readback (GLFence::create + clientWait) is
    unchanged.
  - NonCompositedFrameRenderer (WPE only in 2.52) shares this target and gets
    the same treatment; its damage is in the same space as its surface.

--- a/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/CoordinatedGraphics/AcceleratedSurface.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/CoordinatedGraphics/AcceleratedSurface.cpp
@@ -54,6 +54,10 @@
 #include <GLES2/gl2.h>
 #endif
 
+#ifndef GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH
+#define GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH 0x0D02
+#endif
+
 #if USE(GBM)
 #include <WebCore/DRMDeviceManager.h>
 #include <WebCore/GBMVersioning.h>
@@ -513,7 +517,32 @@
 
 void AcceleratedSurface::RenderTargetSHMImage::didRenderFrame(Vector<IntRect, 1>&& damageRects)
 {
-    glReadPixels(0, 0, m_bitmap->size().width(), m_bitmap->size().height(), GL_BGRA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, m_bitmap->mutableSpan().data());
+    const IntRect bitmapRect({ }, m_bitmap->size());
+    auto rect = m_changedRect ? intersection(*m_changedRect, bitmapRect) : bitmapRect;
+
+    // Reading a rectangle narrower than the bitmap needs GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH, which OpenGL ES 2 lacks.
+    // Full rows can always be read in place.
+    bool needsRowLength = rect.width() != bitmapRect.width();
+    if (needsRowLength) {
+        auto* context = GLContextWrapper::currentContext();
+        if (!context || context->glVersion() < 300) {
+            rect.setX(0);
+            rect.setWidth(bitmapRect.width());
+            needsRowLength = false;
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (!rect.isEmpty()) {
+        static constexpr size_t bytesPerPixel = 4;
+        const size_t bytesPerRow = m_bitmap->bytesPerRow();
+        auto pixels = m_bitmap->mutableSpan().subspan(rect.y() * bytesPerRow + rect.x() * bytesPerPixel);
+        if (needsRowLength)
+            glPixelStorei(GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH, static_cast<GLint>(bytesPerRow / bytesPerPixel));
+        glReadPixels(rect.x(), rect.y(), rect.width(), rect.height(), GL_BGRA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, pixels.data());
+        if (needsRowLength)
+            glPixelStorei(GL_PACK_ROW_LENGTH, 0);
+    }
+
     RenderTargetShareableBuffer::didRenderFrame(WTF::move(damageRects));
 }
 
@@ -1073,6 +1102,22 @@
 
     Vector<IntRect, 1> damageRects;
 #if ENABLE(DAMAGE_TRACKING)
+    // The damage record of the target covers everything that changed since the target was last
+    // presented. Without frame damage for this frame the records are not maintained, and the whole
+    // target counts as changed.
+    std::optional<IntRect> changedRect;
+    if (std::exchange(m_hasFrameDamage, false)) {
+        addFrameDamageToTargets();
+        if (const auto& targetDamage = m_target->damage()) {
+            const auto& size = m_swapChain.size();
+            auto rect = intersection(targetDamage->bounds(), IntRect({ }, size));
+            if (shouldPaintMirrored())
+                rect.setY(size.height() - rect.maxY());
+            changedRect = rect;
+        }
+    }
+    m_target->setChangedRect(WTF::move(changedRect));
+
     // For now, we use bounding box damage for render target damage, as its only 2 consumers so far
     // (CoordinatedBackingStore & ThreadedCompositor) only fetch bounds. Thus having damage with
     // better resolution is pointless as the bounds are the same in such case.
@@ -1089,18 +1134,28 @@
 #if ENABLE(DAMAGE_TRACKING)
 void AcceleratedSurface::setFrameDamage(Damage&& damage)
 {
+    m_hasFrameDamage = true;
+    m_frameDamageAddedToTargets = false;
     if (!damage.isEmpty())
         m_frameDamage = WTF::move(damage);
     else
         m_frameDamage = std::nullopt;
 }
 
-const std::optional<Damage>& AcceleratedSurface::renderTargetDamage()
+void AcceleratedSurface::addFrameDamageToTargets()
 {
+    if (std::exchange(m_frameDamageAddedToTargets, true))
+        return;
+
     // setFrameDamage() stores an empty frame damage as std::nullopt. Passing that on would reset the
     // damage record of every target to "unknown" and make each of them repaint in full.
     if (m_frameDamage)
         m_swapChain.addDamage(m_frameDamage);
+}
+
+const std::optional<Damage>& AcceleratedSurface::renderTargetDamage()
+{
+    addFrameDamageToTargets();
     static std::optional<Damage> nulloptDamage;
     return m_target ? m_target->damage() : nulloptDamage;
 }
